\title{The Arithmetic of Alignment:\\
Gibbs Variational Structure of KL-Regularized Reward Optimization\\
and Its Number-Theoretic Instantiations}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{19 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop, over a finite response space, a complete and exact theory of the
Kullback--Leibler-regularized reward-optimization objective
\[
J(q) \;=\; \E_{y \sim q}[r(y)] \;-\; \beta\,\KL(q \Vert p) \;+\; \gamma\, \E_{x \sim d}[\log q(x)],
\]
the standard objective governing preference-based fine-tuning of generative models, and we
exhibit a systematic dictionary between this theory and classical analytic number theory.

On the structural side we prove an exact Gibbs variational identity
$J(q) = \beta \log Z(\beta) - \beta\,\KL(q \Vert \pi_\beta)$ identifying the unique optimum
$\pi_\beta \propto p\,e^{r/\beta}$ and the optimal value $V(\beta) = \beta\log Z(\beta)$; a
strict \emph{alignment tax} for the pretraining mix-in; exact identifiability of reward models
modulo additive constants together with the implicit-reward inverse; and a \emph{schedule
collapse} theorem showing that an arbitrary finite schedule of alignment steps at arbitrary
temperatures is reproduced by a single step with a rescaled reward sum.

On the analytic side we prove that the curvature of the value curve in the inverse temperature
$t = 1/\beta$ equals the reward variance under the tilted policy,
$\frac{d^2}{dt^2}\log Z(t) = \Var_{\pi_t}(r)$; deduce strict convexity, an annealing inequality,
a variance-flow identity, and a temperature-uniform speed limit with the sharp Popoviciu
constant $1/4$, whose equality case we characterize. We further prove \emph{spectral rigidity}:
the value curve on $\beta > 0$ determines the reward spectrum and nothing finer. We make
rigidity finite: with $n$ known candidate levels, \emph{any} $n$ distinct temperatures suffice,
via a Chebyshev-system (Descartes--Rolle) argument; with unknown levels, three temperatures
provably do not suffice for two atoms.

On the arithmetic side, the Dirichlet reward $r(n) = -\beta s\log n$ on smooth-number response
spaces has aligned policy the truncated zeta distribution $\pi(n) \propto n^{-s}$; the Euler
product becomes statistical independence of prime exponents, the value is additive over primes
with a Mertens-type strict ceiling, divisibility statistics converge to the classical densities
$1 - p^{-s}$, and curvature decomposes additively. The von Mangoldt reward $r(n) = \Lambda(n)$
on $\{1,\dots,N\}$ has value squeezed between $\psi(N)/N$ and $\log N$ with strict lower
inequality, zero-temperature limit $\log P$ for $P$ the largest prime $\le N$, and satisfies a
\textbf{prime discovery theorem}: if $\beta\log N \le \log 2$, the aligned policy emits a prime
power with probability at least $1/2$, and the prime-power mass is monotone in the leash.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} Gibbs variational principle, KL regularization, free energy, reward
spectrum, Chebyshev system, Euler product, von Mangoldt function, truncated zeta distribution.

\section{Introduction}

The dominant paradigm for shaping the behaviour of large generative models is reward-based
fine-tuning under a divergence constraint. One begins with a reference model $p$ obtained by
supervised training, scores candidate outputs with a reward model $r$, and seeks a new model $q$
that scores well without departing too far from $p$. A third term mixes in the original
pretraining objective to guard against regression on general capability. The resulting
objective, over a fixed prompt and a response space $\Omega$, is
\[
J(q) \;=\; \E_{y \sim q}[r(y)] \;-\; \beta\,\KL(q \Vert p) \;+\; \gamma\, \E_{x \sim d}[\log q(x)],
\qquad \beta > 0,\ \gamma \ge 0 .
\]

Our starting observation is that over a finite response space this objective is not merely
approachable but \emph{solvable}, and that the solution carries enough structure to support a
genuine theory: a thermodynamics of alignment, with temperature, free energy, curvature, phase
behaviour, and rigidity.

The second, more surprising observation is that this thermodynamics is the same one that
analytic number theory has practised, under a different name, since Euler and Riemann. The
Riemann zeta function is a partition function; Dirichlet series are exponential moment
generating functions; Euler products are independence statements; the von Mangoldt function is
the natural arithmetic energy. Once the dictionary is set up, alignment theorems and arithmetic
theorems become the same theorems.

\subsection{Notation and standing assumptions}

Throughout, $\Omega$ is a nonempty finite set of responses. A \emph{distribution} on $\Omega$ is
$q : \Omega \to \R$ with $q \ge 0$ and $\sum_y q(y) = 1$; it is \emph{positive} if $q(y) > 0$ for
all $y$. The reference policy $p$ is assumed positive throughout. The reward $r : \Omega \to \R$
is arbitrary. We write
$\KL(q\Vert g) = \sum_y q(y)\log\frac{q(y)}{g(y)}$ and $H(d) = -\sum_y d(y)\log d(y)$. We use
$\beta > 0$ for the KL coefficient and $t = 1/\beta$ for the inverse temperature.

\section{The exact variational structure}

\begin{definition}[Partition function]
For $\beta \neq 0$, set $\displaystyle Z(\beta) = \sum_{y\in\Omega} p(y)\, e^{r(y)/\beta}$. For
$p$ positive, $Z(\beta) > 0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Aligned policy and free energy]
The \emph{aligned} (Gibbs, softmax-tilted) policy is
$\pi_\beta(y) = p(y)e^{r(y)/\beta}/Z(\beta)$, a positive distribution. The \emph{free energy}
or \emph{value curve} is $V(\beta) = \beta\log Z(\beta)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Gibbs' inequality with equality case]\label{lem:gibbs}
For a distribution $q$ and a positive distribution $g$, $\KL(q\Vert g) \ge 0$, with equality iff
$q = g$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Pointwise, $u\log(u/v) \ge u - v$ for $u \ge 0$, $v > 0$, strictly unless $u = v$. Summing over
$\Omega$ and using $\sum q = \sum g = 1$ gives the inequality; strictness at any point where $q$
and $g$ differ propagates to the sum.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact Gibbs variational identity]\label{thm:var}
Let $\beta > 0$, $p$ positive, $q$ a distribution, and
$J_0(q) = \E_q[r] - \beta\,\KL(q\Vert p)$. Then
\[
J_0(q) \;=\; \beta \log Z(\beta) \;-\; \beta\,\KL(q\Vert \pi_\beta).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Expand $\KL(q\Vert\pi_\beta) = \sum_y q(y)\log\frac{q(y)}{p(y)} - \frac{1}{\beta}\sum_y q(y)r(y)
+ \log Z(\beta)$ using the explicit form of $\pi_\beta$ and $\sum_y q(y) = 1$; multiply by
$\beta$ and rearrange.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Variational principle]
$J_0(q) \le V(\beta)$ for every distribution $q$, with equality iff $q = \pi_\beta$; the
inequality is strict off the optimum.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Alignment never hurts]\label{cor:never-hurts}
$\E_p[r] \le V(\beta)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[PTX bound]\label{thm:ptx}
Let $\gamma \ge 0$, let $d$ be a distribution, and
$J(q) = \E_q[r] - \beta\KL(q\Vert p) + \gamma\E_d[\log q]$. Then for every positive $q$,
\[
J(q) \;\le\; \beta\log Z(\beta) - \gamma H(d).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The cross-entropy inequality $-\sum_y d(y)\log q(y) \ge H(d)$ (i.e.\ $\KL(d\Vert q)\ge 0$) bounds
the third term; Theorem~\ref{thm:var} bounds the first two.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Alignment tax]
For $\gamma > 0$ the bound of Theorem~\ref{thm:ptx} is attained only if $q = \pi_\beta$ and
$q = d$ simultaneously. Hence, unless $\pi_\beta = d$, the inequality is strict for every
positive $q$: the deficit is exactly $\beta\KL(q\Vert\pi_\beta) + \gamma\KL(d\Vert q)$.
\end{theorem}

\section{Thermodynamics of the value curve}

\begin{theorem}[Antitonicity]
If $0 < \beta_1 \le \beta_2$ then $V(\beta_2) \le V(\beta_1)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $V(\beta_2) = \E_{\pi_{\beta_2}}[r] - \beta_2\KL(\pi_{\beta_2}\Vert p)$. Since $\KL \ge 0$
and $\beta_1 \le \beta_2$, replacing $\beta_2$ by $\beta_1$ only increases the expression, and
the result is $J_0^{(\beta_1)}(\pi_{\beta_2}) \le V(\beta_1)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Two-sided bracket]\label{thm:bracket}
$\E_p[r] \le V(\beta) \le \max_y r(y)$ for all $\beta > 0$, and $V(\beta) \ge r(y) + \beta\log p(y)$
for every $y \in \Omega$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[No policy collapse: the KL budget]
If $m \le r \le M$ pointwise, then $\beta\cdot\KL(\pi_\beta\Vert p) \le M - m$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Zero-temperature limit]\label{thm:zerotemp}
$\lim_{\beta\to 0^+} V(\beta) = \max_y r(y)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The upper bound is Theorem~\ref{thm:bracket}; for the lower bound apply the pointwise bound at a
maximizer $y^\star$, giving $V(\beta)\ge \max r + \beta\log p(y^\star)\to\max r$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Infinite-temperature limit with rate]\label{thm:hightemp}
If $m \le r \le M$ then $V(\beta) \le m + e^{(M-m)/\beta}\big(\E_p[r] - m\big)$, and hence
$\lim_{\beta\to\infty}V(\beta) = \E_p[r]$.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Curvature is variance}

Set $t = 1/\beta$ and define the exponential moments
$M_k(t) = \sum_y p(y)r(y)^k e^{r(y)t}$, the tilted policy $\pi_t(y)\propto p(y)e^{r(y)t}$, and
\[
\E_{\pi_t}[r] = \frac{M_1(t)}{M_0(t)}, \qquad
\Var_{\pi_t}(r) = \frac{M_2(t)}{M_0(t)} - \left(\frac{M_1(t)}{M_0(t)}\right)^2 .
\]

\begin{lemma}[Moment ladder]\label{lem:ladder}
$M_k' = M_{k+1}$ for every $k \ge 0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[First derivative]
$\dfrac{d}{dt}\log M_0(t) = \E_{\pi_t}[r]$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Curvature identity]\label{thm:curv}
$\dfrac{d^2}{dt^2}\log M_0(t) = \Var_{\pi_t}(r) = \sum_y \pi_t(y)\big(r(y) - \E_{\pi_t}[r]\big)^2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Differentiate $M_1/M_0$ by the quotient rule and use Lemma~\ref{lem:ladder}, obtaining
$M_2/M_0 - (M_1/M_0)^2$; the sum-of-squares form follows by expanding and using
$\sum_y\pi_t(y) = 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Convexity]
$t\mapsto\log M_0(t)$ is convex on $\R$, and strictly convex as soon as $r$ is non-constant.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Annealing inequality]
For $\theta\in[0,1]$,
$\log Z(\theta t_1 + (1-\theta)t_2) \le \theta\log Z(t_1) + (1-\theta)\log Z(t_2)$. In the
temperature variable the relevant midpoint of $\beta_1,\beta_2$ is their \emph{harmonic} mean; a
direct Cauchy--Schwarz proof of the midpoint case, applied to the vectors
$\sqrt{p}\,e^{rt_i/2}$, gives strictness for $t_1\neq t_2$ and non-constant $r$.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{A speed limit for alignment}

\begin{theorem}[Monotone alignment]
$t\mapsto\E_{\pi_t}[r]$ is nondecreasing.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Popoviciu ceiling]\label{thm:pop}
If $m \le r \le M$ then $\Var_{\pi_t}(r) \le (M-m)^2/4$ for all $t$, uniformly in $|\Omega|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Drift bound]\label{thm:drift}
For $t_1\le t_2$, $\;\E_{\pi_{t_2}}[r] - \E_{\pi_{t_1}}[r] \le \frac{(M-m)^2}{4}(t_2-t_1)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Variance-flow identity]
$\displaystyle\int_{t_1}^{t_2}\Var_{\pi_t}(r)\,dt = \E_{\pi_{t_2}}[r] - \E_{\pi_{t_1}}[r]$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\Var_{\pi_t}(r)$ is continuous and is the derivative of $t\mapsto\E_{\pi_t}[r]$ by
Theorem~\ref{thm:curv}; apply the fundamental theorem of calculus.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness of $1/4$]
Let $\Omega = \{0,1\}$, $r$ the indicator reward and $p$ balanced. Then
$\Var_{\pi_t}(r) = e^t/(1+e^t)^2$, equal to $1/4$ at $t=0$, and
$\frac{d}{dt}\big|_{t=0}\E_{\pi_t}[r] = 1/4$. Hence no constant $C < 1/4$ can replace $1/4$ in
Theorem~\ref{thm:pop} or Theorem~\ref{thm:drift}.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Equality analysis]
With $m\le r\le M$, the Popoviciu ceiling is attained at $t$ if and only if $r$ takes only the
two extreme values $m$ and $M$ and $\E_{\pi_t}[r] = (m+M)/2$.
\end{theorem}

\section{Identifiability, composition, and rigidity}

\begin{theorem}[Shift invariance]\label{thm:shift}
For any constant $c$, $r+c$ induces the same aligned policy as $r$; indeed
$Z_{r+c}(\beta) = e^{c/\beta}Z_r(\beta)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Exact identifiability]
For $\beta > 0$ and positive $p$, $\pi_\beta(r_1,p) = \pi_\beta(r_2,p)$ if and only if
$r_1 = r_2 + c$ pointwise for some constant $c$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Sufficiency is Theorem~\ref{thm:shift}. For necessity, equating the policies pointwise and
taking logarithms gives $r_1(y)/\beta - \log Z_1 = r_2(y)/\beta - \log Z_2$, so
$c = \beta\log(Z_1/Z_2)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Implicit reward]
Every positive distribution $q$ is the aligned policy of the reward $\beta\log(q/p)$. Hence
$r\mapsto\pi_\beta(r,p)$ is a bijection from rewards modulo additive constants onto positive
distributions.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Composition adds rewards]\label{thm:compose}
$\pi_\beta\big(r_2, \pi_\beta(r_1,p)\big) = \pi_\beta(r_1+r_2, p)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Temperature--reward rescaling]\label{thm:rescale}
For nonzero $\beta,\beta'$, $\;\pi_\beta\big((\beta/\beta')r, p\big) = \pi_{\beta'}(r,p)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Schedule collapse]\label{thm:collapse}
Let $L = \big((\beta_1,r_1),\dots,(\beta_k,r_k)\big)$ be a finite schedule executed by folding
the alignment map left over $L$ starting from $p$. Then for \emph{any} fixed $\beta\neq 0$ the
resulting policy equals $\pi_\beta\big(\sum_{i=1}^k (\beta/\beta_i) r_i,\ p\big)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $k$, using Theorem~\ref{thm:rescale} to bring each step to the common temperature
and Theorem~\ref{thm:compose} to merge it into the accumulated reward.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No expressive gain from iteration]
The set of policies reachable by an arbitrary finite schedule equals the set reachable in a
single step.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Spectral rigidity}

\begin{definition}[Reward spectrum]
$\displaystyle m(v) = \sum_{y:\,r(y)=v} p(y)$, the reference mass at reward value $v$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Spectral transform]\label{lem:spectral}
$\displaystyle Z(t) = \sum_v m(v)e^{vt}$, summed over the finite set of attained values.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Independence of exponentials on a half-line]\label{lem:indep}
Let $v_1 < \dots < v_n$ be distinct reals with $\sum_j c_j e^{v_j t} = 0$ for all $t > 0$. Then
all $c_j = 0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
On all of $\R$ this is Dedekind's theorem on independence of characters, applied to the
multiplicative monoid of the reals under addition. That argument does not see a half-line, so
we peel instead: divide by $e^{v_n t}$ and let $t\to\infty$, killing every subdominant term and
forcing $c_n = 0$; induct.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Spectral rigidity]\label{thm:rigidity}
If two alignment problems over possibly different finite response spaces satisfy
$Z_1(\beta) = Z_2(\beta)$ for all $\beta > 0$ --- equivalently $V_1 = V_2$ on $\beta > 0$ ---
then $m_1(v) = m_2(v)$ for every $v\in\R$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:spectral} the hypothesis says two finitely supported exponential sums agree on
a half-line; subtract and apply Lemma~\ref{lem:indep} on the union of the two level sets.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Equal value curves force $\max r_1 = \max r_2$ and equal attained-value sets (for positive
references).
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Finite-sample rigidity}

\begin{theorem}[Known levels, arithmetic grid]
Let $v_1,\dots,v_n$ be known and distinct, and let the probes be an arithmetic grid
$t_0, t_0+\tau,\dots,t_0+(n-1)\tau$ with $\tau\neq0$. Then the mass vector is uniquely determined
by the $n$ measured values.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
On an arithmetic grid the system is Vandermonde in the variables $x_j = e^{v_j\tau}$, distinct
since $\exp$ is injective.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exponential sums are a Chebyshev system]\label{thm:cheb}
Let $v_1 < \dots < v_n$ and $t_1 < \dots < t_n$. If $\sum_{j=1}^n c_j e^{v_j t_i} = 0$ for
$i = 1,\dots,n$, then all $c_j = 0$. Equivalently, a nonzero exponential polynomial with $n$
distinct exponents has at most $n-1$ real zeros.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $n$. Multiplying by $e^{-v_1 t}$ preserves the zero set, giving
$g(t) = \sum_j c_j e^{(v_j-v_1)t}$ with $n$ zeros. Rolle's theorem on each of the $n-1$
consecutive intervals yields $n-1$ distinct zeros of $g'$, an exponential polynomial with the
$n-1$ positive strictly increasing exponents $v_2-v_1 < \dots < v_n-v_1$ and coefficients
$c_j(v_j-v_1)$. By induction those coefficients vanish, and positivity of the exponents forces
$c_2 = \dots = c_n = 0$; evaluating at $t_1$ then gives $c_1 = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Audit with known levels]
With $n$ known candidate levels, the reward spectrum is determined by the partition function at
\emph{any} $n$ distinct inverse temperatures.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Three temperatures do not suffice for two unknown atoms]\label{thm:prony}
There exist two alignment problems on $\Omega = \{0,1\}$ with positive references and all four
reward levels pairwise distinct, whose partition functions agree at $t = 0,1,2$ but whose reward
spectra differ.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The two-point distributions on $\{1,3\}$ with masses $(\tfrac12,\tfrac12)$ and on
$\{\tfrac32,4\}$ with masses $(\tfrac45,\tfrac15)$ share mean $2$ and second moment $5$. Take
reward levels equal to the logarithms of the supports, $\{\log 3, 0\}$ and
$\{\log 4, \log\tfrac32\}$, and the reference masses as given. Then
$Z(t) = \sum_j a_j e^{v_j t} = \sum_j a_j x_j^{t}$, so agreement at $t=1,2$ is exactly equality
of the first two moments, and $t=0$ is normalization. The spectra differ at $v = \log 3$.
\end{proof}

\section{Arithmetic instantiation I: Dirichlet rewards and Euler products}

Fix distinct primes $P_1,\dots,P_k$ and caps $A_1,\dots,A_k$, and take
$\Omega = \prod_i\{0,\dots,A_i\}$ with $n(a) = \prod_i P_i^{a_i}$ and uniform reference. Unique
factorization guarantees that distinct tuples name distinct integers.

\begin{definition}[Dirichlet reward]
For a sharpness $s$, set $r(a) = -\beta s \log n(a)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Aligned policy is the truncated zeta law]
$\displaystyle \pi(a) = \frac{n(a)^{-s}}{\sum_{a'} n(a')^{-s}}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Euler product]
With $\zloc(s;P,A) = \sum_{j=0}^A P^{-js}$,
$\displaystyle \sum_{a\in\Omega} n(a)^{-s} = \prod_{i=1}^k \zloc(s;P_i,A_i)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Independence of prime exponents]
Under the aligned policy the coordinates are mutually independent:
$\displaystyle \pi(a) = \prod_{i=1}^k \frac{P_i^{-a_i s}}{\zloc(s;P_i,A_i)}$, each marginal a
truncated geometric law in the exponent.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Additivity of the value]
$\displaystyle V = \beta\Big(\sum_{i=1}^k \log\zloc(s;P_i,A_i) - \log|\Omega|\Big)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Mertens-type strict ceiling]
If $P_i\ge2$ for all $i$, $k\ge1$ and $s>0$, then
$\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^k\log\zloc(s;P_i,A_i) < -\sum_{i=1}^k\log\big(1 - P_i^{-s}\big)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Euler factor in the limit]
For $P\ge2$, $s>0$: $\zloc(s;P,A)\to(1-P^{-s})^{-1}$ as $A\to\infty$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Divisibility statistics]
The probability that a sample is not divisible by $P$ equals $1/\zloc(s;P,A)$; hence the
probability that $P$ divides the sample is strictly less than $P^{-s}$, and the indivisibility
probability converges to $1-P^{-s}$ as $A\to\infty$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Curvature of the local factors]
For $P\ge2$, $A\ge1$, $s\mapsto\log\zloc(s;P,A)$ is strictly convex on $\R$ with
$\frac{d^2}{ds^2}\log\zloc(s;P,A) = \Var(j\log P)$ under the truncated geometric law on
exponents.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Additive curvature decomposition]
The curvature of the truncated Euler product is the sum of the per-prime curvatures.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Strict log-convexity of the truncated zeta function]
For $N\ge2$, $s\mapsto\sum_{n\le N} n^{-s}$ is strictly log-convex on all of $\R$, with curvature
equal to the variance of $\log n$ under the truncated zeta distribution.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The alignment semigroup acts by Dirichlet exponents]
Composing two alignment steps with Dirichlet rewards of sharpness $s_1$ and $s_2$, at arbitrary
temperatures, yields exactly the truncated zeta policy of sharpness $s_1+s_2$, independently of
the schedule.
\end{theorem}

\section{Arithmetic instantiation II: the von Mangoldt reward}

Let $\Omega = \{1,\dots,N\}$ with uniform reference and reward
\[
\Lambda(n) = \begin{cases}\log P, & n = P^k,\ P\ \text{prime},\ k\ge1,\\ 0,&\text{otherwise},\end{cases}
\qquad \psi(N) = \sum_{n\le N}\Lambda(n).
\]

\begin{theorem}[Explicit aligned policy]
The unnormalized weight of $n$ is $e^{\Lambda(n)/\beta}$, i.e.\ $P^{1/\beta}$ if $n = P^k$ is a
prime power and $1$ otherwise.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Value squeeze]
For $\beta>0$ and $N\ge1$, $\;\psi(N)/N \le V(\beta) \le \log N$, and for $N\ge2$ the left
inequality is strict.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The lower bound is Corollary~\ref{cor:never-hurts}, the uniform reference having expected reward
$\psi(N)/N$; the upper bound is Theorem~\ref{thm:bracket} with $\max_n\Lambda(n)\le\log N$.
Strictness follows because the aligned policy differs from the reference exactly when the reward
is non-constant, and $\Lambda$ is non-constant on $\{1,\dots,N\}$ for $N\ge2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Endpoints are prime-counting data]
For $N\ge2$, $\;\lim_{\beta\to0^+}V(\beta) = \log P_{\max}(N)$ and
$\lim_{\beta\to\infty}V(\beta) = \psi(N)/N$, where $P_{\max}(N)$ is the largest prime $\le N$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Theorems~\ref{thm:zerotemp} and~\ref{thm:hightemp}, with
$\max_{n\le N}\Lambda(n) = \log P_{\max}(N)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Prime discovery]\label{thm:primediscovery}
Let $N\ge2$ and $\beta>0$ satisfy $\beta\log N \le \log 2$. Then
\[
\sum_{\substack{n\le N\\ n\ \text{a prime power}}}\pi_\beta(n) \;\ge\; \tfrac12 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Split the total weight $T$ into the prime-power part $S$ and the remainder $R$. Every
non-prime-power has $\Lambda = 0$ and hence weight $1$, so $R \le N$. The single response $n=2$
contributes $e^{\log2/\beta} = 2^{1/\beta}$, and the hypothesis is exactly
$\log N \le \log2/\beta$, i.e.\ $2^{1/\beta}\ge N$. Hence $S \ge N \ge R$, so $T \le 2S$ and
$S/T \ge 1/2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Monotone level-set mass]
For any threshold $c$, the aligned probability of $\{y : r(y)\ge c\}$ is antitone in $\beta$; in
particular the prime-power mass under the von Mangoldt reward is nondecreasing as the leash is
tightened.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A rearrangement inequality on unnormalized weights: for $y$ in the level set, $z$ outside, and
$\beta_1\le\beta_2$, $(r(y)-r(z))(1/\beta_2 - 1/\beta_1)\le0$, hence
$w_{\beta_2}(y)w_{\beta_1}(z) \le w_{\beta_1}(y)w_{\beta_2}(z)$. Summing over $A\times A^c$ gives
$S(\beta_2)R(\beta_1)\le S(\beta_1)R(\beta_2)$, i.e.\ monotonicity of $S/(S+R)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Downward-closed prime regime]
The set of leash values at which the model is at least half supported on prime powers is
downward closed and contains $\big(0, \log2/\log N\big]$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Arithmetic rigidity of the value curve]
Any reward model on $\{1,\dots,N\}$ with uniform reference whose value curve coincides with that
of $\Lambda$ has, for every $v$, exactly as many responses of value $v$ as $\Lambda$ does. The
curve therefore encodes the multiset $\{\Lambda(1),\dots,\Lambda(N)\}$, equivalently the counts
$\#\{k\ge1 : P^k\le N\}$ for each prime $P$.
\end{theorem}

\section{Synthesis: the dictionary}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}ll@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Alignment concept} & \textbf{Arithmetic counterpart}\\
\midrule
Partition function $Z(t)$ & Dirichlet series / truncated zeta\\
Inverse temperature $t = 1/\beta$ & Dirichlet exponent $s$\\
Aligned (Gibbs) policy & Truncated zeta distribution $\pi(n)\propto n^{-s}$\\
Factorization of the policy & Euler product\\
Independence of coordinates & Multiplicativity\\
Value additivity over components & Logarithm of the Euler product\\
Curvature of the value curve & Variance of $\log n$ under the zeta law\\
Reward spectrum & Value multiset with reference weights\\
Reward maximum & $\log$ of the largest prime $\le N$\\
Reference mean reward & Chebyshev average $\psi(N)/N$\\
Composition of alignment steps & Addition of Dirichlet exponents\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Each row is a theorem, not an analogy. The unifying reason is structural: both sides study the
same object --- an exponentially tilted measure on a finite set --- from opposite ends.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{(A) Value-curve evaluation and aligned sampling.}
Given $(r,p,\beta)$, compute $Z(\beta)$ in $O(|\Omega|)$ operations with the standard max-shift
for numerical stability (factor out $e^{\max r/\beta}$), then $V(\beta) = \beta\log Z(\beta)$ and
$\pi_\beta$ by normalization. Sampling costs $O(\log|\Omega|)$ after an $O(|\Omega|)$ prefix-sum
build.

\paragraph{(B) Spectrum recovery from probes (known levels).}
Given known levels $v_1<\dots<v_n$ and measurements $Z(t_1),\dots,Z(t_n)$ at any $n$ distinct
temperatures, solve $\big(e^{v_j t_i}\big)_{ij}\,a = Z$ for the mass vector $a$. By
Theorem~\ref{thm:cheb} the matrix is invertible for \emph{every} choice of distinct probes, but
conditioning degrades rapidly when probes cluster or levels approach each other. Cost $O(n^3)$
in general, $O(n^2)$ by exploiting Vandermonde structure on an arithmetic grid.

\paragraph{(C) Prime-regime certification.}
Compute $\beta^\star = \log2/\log N$. Theorem~\ref{thm:primediscovery} certifies prime-power mass
$\ge \tfrac12$ for every $\beta\le\beta^\star$, and monotonicity means a single evaluation at
$\beta^\star$ bounds the whole regime below it. Cost $O(N)$ to sieve $\Lambda$, $O(N)$ per
evaluation.

\paragraph{(D) Schedule compilation.}
Given $\big((\beta_i,r_i)\big)_{i\le k}$, emit the single equivalent pair
$\big(\beta, \sum_i(\beta/\beta_i)r_i\big)$ in $O(k|\Omega|)$ operations; by
Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse} this reproduces the schedule endpoint exactly.

\section{Applications and interpretation}

\paragraph{Auditing.} Theorem~\ref{thm:rigidity} says an auditor who can measure the achievable
value at each leash setting learns the reward spectrum exactly and nothing else --- a capability
result for the auditor and a privacy result for the model owner. Theorems~\ref{thm:cheb}
and~\ref{thm:prony} quantify the cost: with known levels the audit is finite and robust to probe
placement; with unknown levels, small probe budgets are provably inconclusive.

\paragraph{Hyperparameter theory.} Theorem~\ref{thm:drift} is a design constraint: whatever the
reward and however large the vocabulary, alignment progress per unit of inverse temperature is
capped by $(M-m)^2/4$, with equality exactly for two-valued rewards at balance. Binary
preference rewards, precisely because they are extreme-valued, are the fastest-moving.

\paragraph{Pipeline design.} Schedule collapse is a negative result for multi-stage alignment as
a \emph{representational} device: nothing new becomes reachable. Any advantage of multi-stage
pipelines must be explained by optimization landscape, sample efficiency, or reward-model
staleness.

\paragraph{Reward hacking, reconsidered.} Theorem~\ref{thm:primediscovery} is a ``reward hacking
works'' theorem. The lesson is not that reward hacking is benign but that it is
\emph{predictable}: the structure of $\pi_\beta$ is completely determined by the reward, and a
characterization of the reward's level sets characterizes the hacked behaviour in advance.

\section{Limitations and future work}

The theory is exact but finite: $\Omega$ is a finite response space, so no statement is made
about infinite-sequence spaces, nor about the optimization difficulty of \emph{reaching}
$\pi_\beta$ within a parametric family. The prompt variable is suppressed; results should be
read conditionally on a fixed prompt.

Promising directions: (i) prompt-averaged rigidity --- does the averaged value curve determine
per-prompt spectra or only their mixture? (ii) sharp probe counts for unknown levels beyond
$n=2$; (iii) arithmetic rewards driving alignment onto sparser sets (twin primes, supports of
multiplicative functions) and their thresholds; (iv) rates and sharpness of the prime-power
phase transition in $N$; (v) curvature as a training diagnostic, using the variance-flow
identity to infer reward structure from observed drift.

\section{Conclusion}

The KL-regularized alignment objective is an exactly solvable model. Its optimum is a Gibbs
measure, its optimal value a free energy, its curvature a variance, and its temperature curve
determines the reward spectrum precisely and nothing more. Once one grants that, the arithmetic
follows without effort: Dirichlet rewards produce zeta policies, Euler products become
independence, and a von Mangoldt reward drives an aligned model onto the primes below an
explicit and computable threshold. Setting up the dictionary once makes each field's theorems
available to the other.

